Final Reflection:
During this project, the architectural choice that mattered most was not depth alone, but the ability to train stably and generalize on a small dataset. Our simple CNN already achieved very high validation accuracy, but the results showed that training dynamics (stability, convergence behavior, and sensitivity) were more informative than accuracy alone due to the small validation/test splits.
In optimization, Adam consistently provided strong and reliable performance with minimal tuning, while SGD with momentum was highly sensitive to the learning rate and behaved differently across architectures. This highlighted the practical importance of hyperparameter sensitivity: the “same” optimizer can succeed or fail depending on both the network capacity and the selected LR.
Batch Normalization improved generalization and robustness. While the best validation accuracy remained similar, the BN model achieved better test accuracy and smoother behavior overall, indicating reduced overfitting and improved stability.
Regularization results were subtle because the dataset was relatively easy for the chosen split. Weight decay and dropout did not change final accuracy, but they improved training stability. In contrast, strong data augmentation sometimes reduced test performance, suggesting that aggressive augmentation can distort relevant cues when data is limited.
Transfer learning provided the clearest practical advantage. Pretraining on CIFAR-10 led to faster convergence on the target dataset, but did not improve the final test accuracy in our split (test ≈ 0.90), likely due to domain mismatch and limited target data. Using a pretrained ResNet50 (ImageNet) was the strongest approach: training only the classifier head already reached excellent performance, and fine-tuning the top layers achieved perfect performance without needing extensive training.
With more time and data, the first improvement would be to increase dataset size and strengthen evaluation (larger validation/test, cross-validation, and additional metrics such as confusion matrix and calibration). I would also explore milder augmentation policies, and systematically tune discriminative learning rates for fine-tuning to avoid over-regularization.
Overall, the main lesson is that in small datasets, the most valuable outcomes are understanding stability, sensitivity, and generalization — not maximizing accuracy on a single split.